  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nAt 07Z, water vapor imagery and H7 analysis showed a mid-level ridge \ncentered over TX/OK/NM with a few shortwaves on its northern \nperiphery. A batch of showers and storms had developed ahead of one \nwave over western KS/NE. The environment ahead of the wave in central \nKS featured 500-1000 J/kg of MUCAPE, 7.0-7.5 C/km mid-level lapse \nrates, and weak shear. Further east, instability was minimal and \nlapse rates were less impressive. Thinking is that showers will \nexpand as the system slowly moves east through the morning. \nThunderstorm chances remain highest in central KS given the \naforementioned parameters, with a general weaken trend expected into \neastern KS later this morning. The most likely hazard with storms \nwill be heavy rainfall. Forecast soundings show a fairly saturated \ncolumn and pwats in the 1.9-2.0 inch range in more central portions \nof the state. QPF is around an inch of rain in central/north-central\nKS, and lower amounts eastward where lift and instability will be \nweaker. Rain and residual cloud cover will keep temperatures on the \ncool side of average today with highs in the upper 70s and low 80s.\n\nFocus then turns to another round of rain expected tonight as \nanother shortwave trough axis moves over the area. A similar \nenvironment will be in place, except that pwats are expected to be a \nbit higher due to stronger low level moisture transport and theta-e \nvalues. Overall lift looks somewhat weak, which leads to some \nuncertainty regarding how widespread precipitation will become, but \nthere is at least potential for heavy rainfall leading to localized \nflooding, especially in locations that receive rain today. The axis \nfor heaviest rainfall still looks to be along and north of I-70 with \nFriday's round, where the HREF and NBM both have about a 50-70% \nchance for at least one inch of rain. Rain should taper off from \nwest to east through midday, with possibly just a few lingering \nshowers by mid-afternoon.\n\nThis weekend, the mid-level ridge axis will quickly expand back to \nthe north. Heat and humidity will respond with highs back in the 90s \nand heat index values exceeding triple digits for most. The hottest \ndays look to be Sunday and Monday before the ridge possibly weakens \nas perturbations rotate around the northern edge. Those \nperturbations are difficult to time at this range, but they could \nbring some rain chances back to the forecast by the middle of next \nweek.",
